  "query_preview": "..Moisture transport is underway, featuring a 20-30kt \nlow-level jet pointed straight up the Gulf of California and into \nthe Lower Colorado River Valley. This advection is due to a deepening \ntrough along most of the west coast, as well as sub-tropical jet and \ns/wv now located around 29N/120W. This will all come together in the \nnext few hours and will amplify throughout the day, before tapering \noff to the east later this evening.\n\nPW values are very high for this time of year and the rapid influx \nof higher dew points across the deserts will quickly destabilize the \natmosphere. As the s/wv approaches, we expect large-scale ascent and \nincreased shear, leading to a risk of isolated severe weather. Much \nof the severe threat will be south of the Mogollon Rim and into the \ncentral deserts, closer in proximity to steep, low-level lapse rates \nand higher boundary layer moisture. Isolated rotating storms may \nproduce large hail and severe wind gusts today in these areas.\n\nAs far as overall rainfall, we expect fairly robust totals through \nthis evening, mainly over Yavapai, western Coconino and northern \nGila counties. These areas are where some upslope may develop, as \nwell as persistent and training storms. Farther to the north and \nnortheast, dynamics, moisture transport and upslope are not nearly \nas impressive and totals will drop off quickly heading toward the \nFour Corners. Within the initial FFA location today, generalized \naccumulations of 0.50 to 2.00\" are likely, with isolated higher \namounts. Due to already saturated ground, this will be more than \nenough to elevate flooding threats by this afternoon.\n\nAs the s/wv move east this evening, forcing, instability and shear \ndrops off in a hurry. By daybreak Saturday through early Saturday \nevening, we do not expect much more than isolated rain showers.\n\nBy later Saturday evening, this is when the main event begins. The \nmain trough along the west coast starts to move southeastward as a \nsignificant plume of sub-tropical moisture and s/wv is entrained \naround 28N/122W. This air mass will be near records as far as PW is \nconcerned for this time of year and the phasing of the two systems \nwill allow for an extended period of moderate to heavy rainfall.\n\nThe threat for severe weather with the second system have mostly \ndiminished, thanks largely to a fully saturated and somewhat cold \ncolumn over the state of Arizona. However, later on Sunday and \nSunday night when the parent trough moves through, there could \nbe some isolated severe along the cold front.\n\nThe period of precipitation for the second event will stretch from \nSaturday evening through Monday morning, allowing plenty of time for \ntotals to add up. The sweet spot, as of now, looks to be just east \nof the I-17 corridor, where as much as 4-5 inches of rainfall could \nfall with the heavier bands. Even within the areas that are not as \nphased and not seeing as much of an upslope component, as much as 2 \ninches may accumulate. This is all on the heels of the rainfall from \nFriday, so more than enough to cause serious hydrological issues. A \nsecond FFA was issued for the entire CWA during this time frame and \nthere are no issues with that.\n\nBehind the final trough passage Monday morning, we quickly dry out \nwith strong ridging developing for a few days. This will be the time \nto catch your breath, as models are hinting at another cut-off low \nforming near the end of next week.\n\nThis is a dangerous situation, especially pertaining to flooding. \nBoth flash flooding and then longer term river flooding will be a \nproblem. Larger stem waterways may be flooding for days after the \nevent, especially the Little Colorado River and the Verde River.\n \nDon't dismiss the severe threat for Friday either, especially south \nof the Mogollon Rim. Stay tuned to your favorite weather source and \nfind a way to receive warnings.",
